      },
      "source": [
        "# Introduction to Graph Neural Networks for Recommender Systems"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "intro_markdown_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "Recommender Systems are vital tools for navigating vast amounts of information by suggesting relevant items to users. Collaborative Filtering (CF) is a common approach, which makes predictions based on past user-item interactions (e.g., ratings, purchases, clicks).\n",
        "\n",
        "User-item interactions can be naturally represented as a **bipartite graph**, where one set of nodes represents users, another set represents items, and edges connect users to items they have interacted with. This graph structure inherently captures the relationships between users and items.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Graph Neural Networks (GNNs)** are a class of deep learning models designed to operate directly on graph-structured data. They learn node representations (embeddings) by aggregating information from their neighbors. This makes them particularly well-suited for recommender systems built on user-item interaction graphs.\n",
        "\n",
        "GNNs have the potential to capture **higher-order relationships**. For instance, two users might be considered similar not just because they rated the same item, but because they rated items that were themselves rated by a similar set of other users. GNNs can learn these complex patterns through message passing over multiple hops in the graph.\n",
        "\n",
        "**LightGCN** (He et al., SIGIR 2020) is an example of a simple yet effective GNN model for recommendation. It simplifies traditional GCNs by removing feature transformations and non-linear activation functions in the propagation layers, focusing on neighborhood aggregation.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Tutorial Outline:**\n",
        "1.  Setup necessary libraries.\n",
        "2.  Load and preprocess the MovieLens 100k dataset.\n",
        "3.  Construct a user-item interaction graph suitable for GNNs.\n",
        "4.  Understand and implement a LightGCN-inspired GNN model.\n",
        "5.  Train the GNN model to predict ratings.\n",
        "6.  Generate recommendations and perform basic evaluation.\n",
        "7.  Conclude and discuss further steps."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "setup_markdown_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Setup\n",
        "\n",
        "This section installs and imports the necessary libraries. We'll use PyTorch and PyTorch Geometric (PyG) for building the GNN."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "setup_code_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "!pip install torch torch-scatter torch-sparse torch-cluster torch-spline-conv torch-geometric pandas numpy scikit-learn scipy --upgrade\n",
        "\n",
        "import torch\n",
        "import torch.nn as nn\n",
        "import torch.nn.functional as F\n",
        "import torch.optim as optim\n",
        "from torch_geometric.nn.conv import MessagePassing\n",
        "from torch_geometric.data import Data\n",
        "from torch_geometric.utils import degree\n",
        "import torch_geometric\n",
        "\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\n",
        "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
        "\n",
        "import os\n",
        "import urllib.request\n",
        "import zipfile\n",
        "from matplotlib import pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"PyTorch version: {torch.__version__}\")\n",
        "print(f\"PyG version: {torch_geometric.__version__}\")"
      ],
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "data_loading_markdown_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Data Loading and Preprocessing\n",
        "\n",
        "We will use the MovieLens 100k dataset. It contains 100,000 ratings from 943 users for 1682 movies."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "data_loading_code_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "# Download and extract MovieLens 100k\n",
        "url = \"http://files.grouplens.org/datasets/movielens/ml-100k.zip\"\n",
        "zip_path = \"ml-100k.zip\"\n",
        "extract_path = \"ml-100k\"\n",
        "\n",
        "if not os.path.exists(extract_path):\n",
        "    print(\"Downloading MovieLens 100k...\")\n",
        "    urllib.request.urlretrieve(url, zip_path)\n",
        "    print(\"Extracting...\")\n",
        "    with zipfile.ZipFile(zip_path, \"r\") as zip_ref:\n",
        "        zip_ref.extractall(\".\")\n",
        "    # os.remove(zip_path) # Keep the zip for inspection if needed\n",
        "    print(\"Dataset downloaded and extracted.\")\n",
        "else:\n",
        "    print(\"MovieLens 100k dataset already present.\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Load u.data file\n",
        "columns = ['user_id', 'item_id', 'rating', 'timestamp']\n",
        "df_ratings = pd.read_csv(os.path.join(extract_path, 'u.data'), sep='\\t', names=columns)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\nRatings data sample:\")\n",
        "print(df_ratings.head())"
      ],
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "preprocessing_markdown_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "### User and Item ID Encoding\n",
        "GNN embedding layers require node IDs to be contiguous integers starting from 0."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "preprocessing_code_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "user_encoder = LabelEncoder()\n",
        "item_encoder = LabelEncoder()\n",
        "\n",
        "df_ratings['user_encoded_id'] = user_encoder.fit_transform(df_ratings['user_id'])\n",
        "df_ratings['item_encoded_id'] = item_encoder.fit_transform(df_ratings['item_id'])\n",
        "\n",
        "num_users = len(user_encoder.classes_)\n",
        "num_items = len(item_encoder.classes_)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"\\nNumber of unique users: {num_users}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Number of unique items: {num_items}\")\n",
        "print(\"\\nProcessed ratings data with encoded IDs:\")\n",
        "print(df_ratings.head())"
      ],
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "graph_construction_markdown_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Graph Construction\n",
        "We create a bipartite graph where users and items are nodes. An edge exists if a user has rated an item. For this tutorial, we'll consider all interactions as edges. Item nodes will be offset by `num_users` to distinguish them from user nodes in the graph's global indexing scheme (i.e., user nodes: `0` to `num_users-1`; item nodes: `num_users` to `num_users + num_items - 1`)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "graph_construction_code_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "# User nodes are [0, num_users-1]\n",
        "# Item nodes are [num_users, num_users + num_items - 1]\n",
        "user_nodes = df_ratings['user_encoded_id'].values\n",
        "item_nodes_shifted = df_ratings['item_encoded_id'].values + num_users # Shift item IDs\n",
        "\n",
        "# Create edges in both directions for undirected message passing (user-item and item-user)\n",
        "edge_index_user_to_item = torch.tensor([user_nodes, item_nodes_shifted], dtype=torch.long)\n",
        "edge_index_item_to_user = torch.tensor([item_nodes_shifted, user_nodes], dtype=torch.long)\n",
        "edge_index = torch.cat([edge_index_user_to_item, edge_index_item_to_user], dim=1)\n",
        "\n",
        "# For this explicit rating prediction task, we'll store ratings as edge attributes\n",
        "ratings_tensor = torch.tensor(df_ratings['rating'].values, dtype=torch.float)\n",
        "# Duplicate ratings for the reversed edges to align with edge_index\n",
        "edge_attr_ratings = torch.cat([ratings_tensor, ratings_tensor], dim=0)\n",
        "\n",
        "graph_data = Data(edge_index=edge_index, edge_attr=edge_attr_ratings, \n",
        "                    num_nodes=num_users + num_items)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\nPyG Graph Data Object:\")\n",
        "print(graph_data)\n",
        "print(f\"Number of nodes: {graph_data.num_nodes}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Number of edges: {graph_data.num_edges}\")"
      ],
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "lightgcn_model_markdown_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "## LightGCN-inspired GNN Model\n",
        "\n",
        "### Explanation\n",
        "\n",
        "LightGCN simplifies traditional Graph Convolutional Networks (GCNs) for recommendation. Its core idea is to learn user and item embeddings by linearly propagating them over the user-item interaction graph.\n",
        "\n",
        "Key aspects:\n",
        "1.  **Embedding Layer**: Users and items are initially represented by learnable embedding vectors. These are stored in a single embedding table where the first `num_users` rows are for users and the next `num_items` rows are for items.\n",
        "2.  **Message Passing (Propagation Rule)**: In each GNN layer, a node's embedding is updated by aggregating the embeddings of its neighbors from the previous layer. LightGCN uses a simple weighted sum of neighbor embeddings, normalized by node degrees (specifically, symmetric normalization: $1/(\\sqrt{D_u D_v})$ where $D_u$ and $D_v$ are degrees of nodes $u$ and $v$). Crucially, it does *not* use feature transformation matrices (like weight matrices in GCN layers) or non-linear activation functions (like ReLU) during propagation. This makes it \"light.\"\n",
        "    The propagation rule for a node $u$ at layer $k+1$ can be written as:\n",
        "    $e_{u}^{(k+1)} = \\sum_{i \\in N(u)} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|N(u)|}\\sqrt{|N(i)|}} e_{i}^{(k)}$\n",
        "    where $e_u^{(k)}$ is the embedding of node $u$ at layer $k$, and $N(u)$ is the set of neighbors of $u$.\n",
        "3.  **Layer Combination**: The final embedding for a node is a combination of its embeddings from all layers (including the initial embedding layer, layer 0). A common approach is to take a weighted average or sum. For this tutorial, we'll use the mean of the embeddings from all layers.\n",
        "4.  **Prediction**: For a user-item pair, the predicted score (e.g., rating) is the dot product of their final combined embeddings."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "lightgcn_model_code_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "class LightGCNRec(MessagePassing):\n",
        "    def __init__(self, num_users, num_items, embedding_dim=64, num_layers=3, **kwargs):\n",
        "        super().__init__(aggr='add', **kwargs) # 'add' for sum aggregation\n",
        "        self.num_users = num_users\n",
        "        self.num_items = num_items\n",
        "        self.embedding_dim = embedding_dim\n",
        "        self.num_layers = num_layers\n",
        "\n",
        "        # User and item embeddings are stored in one table\n",
        "        self.embeddings = nn.Embedding(num_users + num_items, embedding_dim)\n",
        "        nn.init.xavier_uniform_(self.embeddings.weight) # Xavier initialization\n",
        "\n",
        "    def forward(self, edge_index):\n",
        "        # Get initial embeddings (layer 0)\n",
        "        # The .weight attribute of nn.Embedding gives the actual tensor of embeddings\n",
        "        all_layer_embeddings = [self.embeddings.weight] \n",
        "\n",
        "        current_layer_embeddings = self.embeddings.weight\n",
        "        for _ in range(self.num_layers):\n",
        "            # Message passing: propagate and aggregate\n",
        "            propagated_embeddings = self.propagate(edge_index, x=current_layer_embeddings)\n",
        "            current_layer_embeddings = propagated_embeddings\n",
        "            all_layer_embeddings.append(current_layer_embeddings)\n",
        "        \n",
        "        # Combine embeddings from all layers (mean of embeddings from layer 0 to num_layers)\n",
        "        final_embeddings_stack = torch.stack(all_layer_embeddings, dim=1)\n",
        "        final_embeddings = torch.mean(final_embeddings_stack, dim=1)\n",
        "        \n",
        "        user_final_embeddings = final_embeddings[:self.num_users]\n",
        "        item_final_embeddings = final_embeddings[self.num_users:] # Item embeddings start after user embeddings\n",
        "        \n",
        "        return user_final_embeddings, item_final_embeddings\n",
        "\n",
        "    def message(self, x_j, edge_index_i, edge_index_j, size_i, size_j):\n",
        "        # x_j contains embeddings of source nodes (neighbors)\n",
        "        # Symmetric normalization as in LightGCN paper\n",
        "        # edge_index_i are target nodes, edge_index_j are source nodes for these messages\n",
        "        deg_i = degree(edge_index_i, num_nodes=size_i, dtype=x_j.dtype) # Degree of target nodes\n",
        "        deg_j = degree(edge_index_j, num_nodes=size_j, dtype=x_j.dtype) # Degree of source nodes\n",
        "\n",
        "        deg_i_inv_sqrt = deg_i.pow(-0.5)\n",
        "        deg_j_inv_sqrt = deg_j.pow(-0.5)\n",
        "        \n",
        "        # Fill NaN or inf resulting from 0 degrees (isolated nodes, though unlikely in connected CF graph)\n",
        "        deg_i_inv_sqrt[deg_i_inv_sqrt == float('inf')] = 0\n",
        "        deg_j_inv_sqrt[deg_j_inv_sqrt == float('inf')] = 0\n",
        "        \n",
        "        # norm for edge (j,i) is 1/ (sqrt(deg_j) * sqrt(deg_i))\n",
        "        # We access degrees using the original node indices from edge_index\n",
        "        norm = deg_j_inv_sqrt[edge_index_j] * deg_i_inv_sqrt[edge_index_i]\n",
        "        \n",
        "        return norm.view(-1, 1) * x_j\n",
        "\n",
        "    def predict_ratings(self, user_embeddings, item_embeddings, user_indices, item_indices):\n",
        "        # Ensure indices are for the respective embedding tables (0 to num_users-1, 0 to num_items-1)\n",
        "        user_emb = user_embeddings[user_indices]\n",
        "        item_emb = item_embeddings[item_indices]\n",
        "        ratings = torch.sum(user_emb * item_emb, dim=1) # Dot product\n",
        "        return ratings\n",
        "\n",
        "# Instantiate the model\n",
        "embedding_dimension = 64\n",
        "n_gnn_layers = 2 \n",
        "gnn_model = LightGCNRec(num_users, num_items, embedding_dim=embedding_dimension, num_layers=n_gnn_layers)\n",
        "print(\"\\nGNN Model Architecture:\")\n",
        "print(gnn_model)"
      ],
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "training_markdown_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Model Training\n",
        "\n",
        "### Loss Function\n",
        "Since we are predicting explicit ratings (1-5 stars from MovieLens), we will use the **Mean Squared Error (MSE)** loss. This measures the average squared difference between the predicted ratings and the actual ratings. The graph edges contain the original ratings.\n",
        "\n",
        "The training data will consist of (user, item, rating) triples from the dataset."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "training_code_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "# Prepare training data: user_indices, item_indices (original encoded, not shifted), and their ratings\n",
        "train_user_indices = torch.tensor(df_ratings['user_encoded_id'].values, dtype=torch.long)\n",
        "train_item_indices = torch.tensor(df_ratings['item_encoded_id'].values, dtype=torch.long)\n",
        "train_actual_ratings = torch.tensor(df_ratings['rating'].values, dtype=torch.float)\n",
        "\n",
        "optimizer = optim.Adam(gnn_model.parameters(), lr=0.01, weight_decay=1e-4) # L2 regularization via weight_decay\n",
        "criterion = nn.MSELoss()\n",
        "\n",
        "num_epochs = 100 # Adjust as needed, may need more for convergence\n",
        "print_interval = 10\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\nStarting GNN model training...\")\n",
        "gnn_model.train() # Set model to training mode\n",
        "for epoch in range(num_epochs):\n",
        "    optimizer.zero_grad()\n",
        "    \n",
        "    # Get final user and item embeddings after GNN layers\n",
        "    # graph_data.edge_index contains edges for message passing\n",
        "    user_final_embeddings, item_final_embeddings = gnn_model(graph_data.edge_index)\n",
        "    \n",
        "    # Predict ratings for all training interactions\n",
        "    # train_user_indices and train_item_indices are used to select the specific embeddings for prediction\n",
        "    predicted_ratings = gnn_model.predict_ratings(user_final_embeddings, item_final_embeddings, \n",
        "                                                train_user_indices, train_item_indices)\n",
        "    \n",
        "    loss = criterion(predicted_ratings, train_actual_ratings)\n",
        "    loss.backward()\n",
        "    optimizer.step()\n",
        "    \n",
        "    if (epoch + 1) % print_interval == 0:\n",
        "        rmse = torch.sqrt(loss).item()\n",
        "        print(f'Epoch {epoch+1}/{num_epochs} - Loss (MSE): {loss.item():.4f}, RMSE: {rmse:.4f}')\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Training complete.\")"
      ],
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "recommendation_markdown_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Generating Recommendations & Basic Evaluation\n",
        "\n",
        "### Explanation\n",
        "To generate recommendations for a specific user:\n",
        "1.  Get the final learned embeddings for the target user and all items from the trained GNN model.\n",
        "2.  Compute the dot product between the user's embedding and every item's embedding. This gives a predicted score/rating for each item.\n",
        "3.  Sort items by these scores in descending order.\n",
        "4.  Optionally, filter out items the user has already interacted with (seen in the training data).\n",
        "5.  Present the top-N items as recommendations.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Basic Evaluation\n",
        "For a simple evaluation in this explicit rating prediction context, we can re-calculate the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) on the training data using the final embeddings. A proper evaluation would use a held-out test set of (user, item, rating) triples not seen during training."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "recommendation_code_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "gnn_model.eval() # Set model to evaluation mode\n",
        "with torch.no_grad(): # Disable gradient calculations for inference\n",
        "    user_final_embeddings, item_final_embeddings = gnn_model(graph_data.edge_index)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Example: Get top 5 recommendations for user_encoded_id = 0\n",
        "target_user_encoded_id = 0\n",
        "target_user_embedding = user_final_embeddings[target_user_encoded_id]\n",
        "\n",
        "# Calculate scores for all items for this user\n",
        "all_item_scores = torch.matmul(item_final_embeddings, target_user_embedding) # Dot product\n",
        "\n",
        "# Get items already rated by this user to exclude them\n",
        "original_user_id_for_recs = user_encoder.inverse_transform([target_user_encoded_id])[0]\n",
        "rated_item_ids_original_for_user = df_ratings[df_ratings['user_id'] == original_user_id_for_recs]['item_id'].values\n",
        "rated_item_encoded_ids_for_user = item_encoder.transform(rated_item_ids_original_for_user)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Set scores of already rated items to a very low value to effectively filter them out\n",
        "all_item_scores[rated_item_encoded_ids_for_user] = -float('inf')\n",
        "\n",
        "top_k = 5\n",
        "top_k_scores, top_k_indices_encoded = torch.topk(all_item_scores, top_k)\n",
        "\n",
        "top_k_item_ids_original = item_encoder.inverse_transform(top_k_indices_encoded.cpu().numpy())\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"\\nTop {top_k} recommendations for original user ID {original_user_id_for_recs} (encoded: {target_user_encoded_id}):\")\n",
        "for i, item_id_orig in enumerate(top_k_item_ids_original):\n",
        "    print(f\"  {i+1}. Item ID {item_id_orig} (Predicted Score: {top_k_scores[i]:.4f})\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Basic Evaluation on the training data (as a proxy for test set in this simple tutorial)\n",
        "predicted_train_ratings = gnn_model.predict_ratings(user_final_embeddings, item_final_embeddings, \n",
        "                                                  train_user_indices, train_item_indices)\n",
        "final_train_mse = criterion(predicted_train_ratings, train_actual_ratings).item()\n",
        "final_train_rmse = np.sqrt(final_train_mse)\n",
        "print(f\"\\nFinal RMSE on training data: {final_train_rmse:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(\"(Note: For robust evaluation, a separate, held-out test set of user-item-rating interactions should be used.)\")"
      ],
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "conclusion_markdown_gnn_v2"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Conclusion\n",
        "\n",
        "In this tutorial, we explored the fundamentals of using Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) for recommender systems. Key takeaways:\n",
        "*   User-item interactions can be effectively modeled as a graph.\n",
        "*   GNNs, like the LightGCN-inspired model we implemented, learn embeddings for users and items by propagating information through this graph structure.\n",
        "*   This message passing allows GNNs to capture complex, higher-order relationships beyond what traditional matrix factorization can typically model.\n",
        "*   We trained our GNN to predict explicit ratings using MSE loss and evaluated it with RMSE.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Further Improvements and Directions:**\n",
        "*   **Implicit Feedback**: Adapt the model for implicit feedback scenarios (e.g., clicks, views where all observed interactions are positive). This typically involves using loss functions like Bayesian Personalized Ranking (BPR) loss or Binary Cross-Entropy (BCE) loss, along with negative sampling strategies.\n",
        "*   **Ranking Evaluation**: For implicit feedback or when the goal is to provide a ranked list, use ranking-based metrics like Precision@k, Recall@k, and Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain (NDCG@k).\n",
        "*   **More Sophisticated GNN Layers**: Explore other GNN architectures like GCN (Graph Convolutional Network), GraphSAGE, or GAT (Graph Attention Network), which might include feature transformations and non-linearities in their propagation steps for richer representations.\n",
        "*   **Content Features**: Incorporate user or item content features (e.g., movie genres, user demographics, item descriptions) into the GNN by augmenting initial node features instead of just learnable embeddings.\n",
        "*   **Scalability**: For very large graphs, techniques like graph sampling (e.g., Cluster-GCN, GraphSAINT) or using more advanced mini-batching strategies provided by PyTorch Geometric become important.\n",
        "*   **Hyperparameter Tuning**: Systematically tune parameters like embedding dimension, number of GNN layers, learning rate, and regularization strength to optimize performance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
